course: dictionary
lesson: 4743
title: Context clues with a biting fly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words in science texts are new, but the words around them can tell you what they mean. «Bitten by a horsefly» tells you that a horsefly is something that can bite, and that a bite happens to skin. Ask yourself what would fit there, then swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: «Horseflies cut the skin with blade-like mouthparts and lap up the blood, so the bite hurts more than a mosquito's.» The word so tells you the pain comes from what the fly does. Blade-like means shaped like a blade, something sharp, so the clues point to a fly that cuts.
explain: Context can also show you how a word is used. The zebra sentence says horseflies «fail to land properly on stripes», so the stripes stop them landing. The words fail and properly are the clues here, and they tell you what the flies do and do not manage.
fact: A horsefly is a large fly.
fact: Only the female horsefly bites people and animals and drinks their blood.
fact: A horsefly bite is painful and can swell and itch.
fact: Horseflies are drawn to dark moving things and warm bodies.
fact: The best-supported idea is that zebra stripes put off biting flies such as horseflies; the idea that they confuse lions has little proof.
exercise: «Horseflies cut the skin with blade-like mouthparts.» What does blade-like suggest? | Sticky, like glue | *Sharp, like a blade | Bright and shiny | Soft and furry
exercise: Which horseflies bite people and drink their blood? | All flies of every kind | Only the males | *Only the females | None of them
exercise: «Its bite is painful and can swell and itch.» What does painful mean? | *Hurts | Pretty | Fun to feel | Quiet and still
test: Which things are horseflies drawn to, according to the sentences? | Cold stones and still water | Bright flowers only | Dry sand and rocks | *Dark moving things and warm bodies
test: In «so the bite hurts more than a mosquito's», what does more than mean? | Less, weaker | *Greater, stronger | The same as | Not at all
exam: Which fact about horseflies is true? | All flies bite and drink blood | *Only females bite and drink blood | Horseflies bite but never drink blood | Horseflies are small and harmless
